1
00:00:00,533 --> 00:00:03,573
If you're a software developer, I want
you to think of the craziest edge cases

2
00:00:03,593 --> 00:00:05,163
in the world. The one
thing that you thought,

3
00:00:05,593 --> 00:00:09,153
oh, yeah, there's no way a user would
navigate and navigate and navigate and

4
00:00:09,173 --> 00:00:10,763
end up in this kind of situation.

5
00:00:11,093 --> 00:00:14,703
I promise you, with everything that
I have and everything that I hope,

6
00:00:14,813 --> 00:00:16,233
someone is going to report an issue.

7
00:00:16,253 --> 00:00:20,282
And it's going to be exactly that
edge case that you never thought of.

8
00:00:20,643 --> 00:00:22,063
It doesn't even have to be something big.

9
00:00:22,083 --> 00:00:26,483
You don't even have to have a lot of
users for edge cases that you didn't

10
00:00:27,038 --> 00:00:31,668
write for to exist. Usually when we
build, we create a direct simple path,

11
00:00:31,688 --> 00:00:32,688
you know?

12
00:00:32,748 --> 00:00:35,928
And just before that, you
know, the user fills a form,

13
00:00:35,948 --> 00:00:37,798
they click a button, and then they wait.

14
00:00:38,180 --> 00:00:40,600
But it sounds straightforward,
but it's not.

15
00:00:40,815 --> 00:00:42,695
Real users don't always do that.

16
00:00:42,715 --> 00:00:46,395
Real users don't just always fill the
form and click the button and wait.

17
00:00:46,640 --> 00:00:48,750
Real users double-click,
like ta-ta-ta-ta-ta-ta-ta.

18
00:00:49,578 --> 00:00:52,368
They click a lot. Real
users open different tabs.

19
00:00:52,388 --> 00:00:54,708
Real users network drop halfway.

20
00:00:54,728 --> 00:00:55,728
Real users

21
00:00:55,868 --> 00:00:59,538
leave the page open for two hours,
and they go and then they come back.

22
00:00:59,828 --> 00:01:01,088
And they're not doing anything strange.

23
00:01:01,168 --> 00:01:05,293
They are just being users. One of the
most common edge cases in production is

24
00:01:05,313 --> 00:01:06,323
the duplicate submission.

25
00:01:06,813 --> 00:01:10,203
If, for example, in an online
shop, someone clicks place order,

26
00:01:10,593 --> 00:01:14,153
and maybe the page feels a little
slow, they click it again.

27
00:01:14,413 --> 00:01:15,893
Now our server gets

28
00:01:16,433 --> 00:01:18,593
two requests, and it creates two orders.

29
00:01:18,933 --> 00:01:21,793
And if we charge on each order,
you can charge the user twice.

30
00:01:21,813 --> 00:01:23,163
It doesn't even need a double-click.

31
00:01:23,493 --> 00:01:26,823
The phone can lose the network after the
request was sent and the app can retry.

32
00:01:27,373 --> 00:01:29,983
Or they can refresh the page, and then
the browser asks them if they want to

33
00:01:30,003 --> 00:01:31,753
send the form again and they say yes.

34
00:01:31,993 --> 00:01:34,993
Disabling the button after the
first click helps with this,

35
00:01:35,013 --> 00:01:36,563
but it only covers the double-click part.

36
00:01:36,583 --> 00:01:39,253
It does nothing for like
maybe a retry or a second tab.

37
00:01:39,393 --> 00:01:42,143
The fix that is common for
this is using an idempotency

38
00:01:42,683 --> 00:01:43,683
key. Idempotence--

39
00:01:44,163 --> 00:01:45,163
like that.

40
00:01:45,233 --> 00:01:46,233
Basically, an idempotency

41
00:01:46,523 --> 00:01:51,693
key puts things in place such that doing
something twice has the same result,

42
00:01:51,713 --> 00:01:53,323
as doing it just once.

43
00:01:54,063 --> 00:01:58,273
Stripe's API uses the same idea
with an idempotency key header.

44
00:01:58,293 --> 00:01:59,293
So a retried

45
00:01:59,673 --> 00:02:01,223
request doesn't create a second payment.

46
00:02:01,353 --> 00:02:03,893
Another common edge case in
production is a race condition.

47
00:02:03,913 --> 00:02:06,423
A race condition is when two
things happen at the same time.

48
00:02:06,453 --> 00:02:09,933
And your code just assume that they
would happen one after the other.

49
00:02:10,102 --> 00:02:11,102
was this researcher

50
00:02:11,132 --> 00:02:15,922
called Egor that found one of these race
conditions in Starbucks gift cards.

51
00:02:16,872 --> 00:02:18,682
So he had gift cards worth $15

52
00:02:19,212 --> 00:02:23,742
in total, and he sent the
same balance transfer twice,

53
00:02:23,762 --> 00:02:26,362
at the same time, from
two different sessions.

54
00:02:26,432 --> 00:02:29,462
Both transfers went through,
and his card ended up with $20

55
00:02:30,112 --> 00:02:33,952
between them. So each request checked the
balance, saw that it was enough money,

56
00:02:33,972 --> 00:02:36,762
and moved it. Neither request was
there for the other one to finish.

57
00:02:37,172 --> 00:02:38,842
The same thing can happen in your app.

58
00:02:38,862 --> 00:02:43,287
For example, in our online shop,
say we have one item left in stock,

59
00:02:43,597 --> 00:02:46,227
two people click buy in
the exact same second,

60
00:02:46,897 --> 00:02:48,607
both requests check the stock,

61
00:02:49,007 --> 00:02:53,577
both see one item left, both create an
order, and now we have sold an item that

62
00:02:53,597 --> 00:02:55,967
we do not have. What do we do about this?

63
00:02:56,357 --> 00:02:59,437
So if the two requests arrive at the
same time, only one of them can take the

64
00:02:59,457 --> 00:03:03,537
last item. The other one gets zero rows
updated, and that's how you know it's

65
00:03:03,557 --> 00:03:06,027
sold out. A lot of edge case

66
00:03:06,717 --> 00:03:11,247
situations in production can also happen
with names because we sometimes build

67
00:03:11,437 --> 00:03:13,537
names featuring like default names.

68
00:03:13,757 --> 00:03:17,477
Take the name O'Brien, for example, the
apostrophe is the same character that

69
00:03:17,497 --> 00:03:19,347
starts an empty piece of text in SQL.

70
00:03:19,497 --> 00:03:22,337
So if your code glues the
name straight into the query,

71
00:03:22,457 --> 00:03:23,397
the apostrophe

72
00:03:23,417 --> 00:03:25,527
ends the text early, and the query breaks.

73
00:03:25,747 --> 00:03:28,517
And if an apostrophe can
break your query by accident,

74
00:03:28,537 --> 00:03:31,237
it means that someone can type
something after the apostrophe

75
00:03:31,677 --> 00:03:32,677
on purpose,

76
00:03:32,797 --> 00:03:34,087
and that will be SQL injection.

77
00:03:34,437 --> 00:03:37,787
In 2016, the BBC wrote about
a woman called Jennifer Null.

78
00:03:37,982 --> 00:03:41,722
When Jennifer Null tries to buy a plane
ticket, most websites tell her that

79
00:03:41,742 --> 00:03:43,872
she has left the surname field blank.

80
00:03:44,162 --> 00:03:48,662
Her surname is Null. The website sees
the name Null and treats it as empty.

81
00:03:48,842 --> 00:03:49,842
And yeah,

82
00:03:49,892 --> 00:03:52,282
so Null, apart from even
being in a name situation,

83
00:03:52,312 --> 00:03:57,222
is also an edge case that catches almost
everyone because Null means no value.

84
00:03:57,322 --> 00:04:00,142
There was another story, I
was told at a conference,

85
00:04:00,442 --> 00:04:01,442
where a security

86
00:04:01,752 --> 00:04:03,092
researcher got a personal

87
00:04:03,652 --> 00:04:05,702
license plate in
California that said NULL.

88
00:04:05,922 --> 00:04:07,662
Like that was the license plate, NULL.

89
00:04:07,842 --> 00:04:10,122
Then he started getting
parking tickets over $12,000

90
00:04:10,722 --> 00:04:12,933
in fines that were not his tickets.

91
00:04:12,953 --> 00:04:14,993
There were tickets where no
plate number had been recorded.

92
00:04:15,013 --> 00:04:16,993
And in the system that
processes the tickets,

93
00:04:17,453 --> 00:04:19,883
those were now linked
to a plate called NULL.

94
00:04:19,903 --> 00:04:21,893
So all of them landed on his plate.

95
00:04:22,123 --> 00:04:23,753
So this is another null situation.

96
00:04:23,933 --> 00:04:27,013
Another one that is common, another thing
that can cause very interesting edge

97
00:04:27,033 --> 00:04:28,983
cases situation in
production is leap years.

98
00:04:29,713 --> 00:04:32,393
Leap years are an edge case that
will show up every four years,

99
00:04:32,413 --> 00:04:33,923
which is exactly why they get missed.

100
00:04:34,963 --> 00:04:36,813
On the 29th of February,

101
00:04:38,013 --> 00:04:39,013
2019,

102
00:04:40,993 --> 00:04:45,563
Microsoft's cloud, Windows Azure, had
an outage because of a leap year bug.

103
00:04:50,578 --> 00:04:52,948
Part of their system made security

104
00:04:53,348 --> 00:04:54,928
certificates that were valid for one year.

105
00:04:55,188 --> 00:04:59,118
And the system worked out the end dates
by taking today's date and just adding

106
00:04:59,138 --> 00:05:00,138
one to the year.

107
00:05:00,483 --> 00:05:05,943
So on the 29th of February, 2012, that
system, that logic gave 29th of February,

108
00:05:06,183 --> 00:05:07,993
2013, and that date doesn't exist.

109
00:05:08,183 --> 00:05:12,643
So creating the certificate failed
and part of Azure went down with it.

110
00:05:12,663 --> 00:05:16,223
So yeah, this is something to think
about as well, leap year bugs.

111
00:05:16,243 --> 00:05:20,223
Another edge case that can cause
issues: time zones and daylight saving.

112
00:05:20,403 --> 00:05:23,963
There's also an edge case in production
that a lot of people do not think about.

113
00:05:23,983 --> 00:05:26,803
In Estonia where I live, the
clock changes twice in a year.

114
00:05:26,903 --> 00:05:30,913
On the 29th of March, the clocks
in Tallinn jump from 3 a.m.

115
00:05:30,933 --> 00:05:32,683
straight to 4 a.m. So 3:30

116
00:05:33,183 --> 00:05:35,973
that morning never happened.
The same way on the 25th

117
00:05:36,343 --> 00:05:37,433
of October,

118
00:05:38,343 --> 00:05:41,143
the clock went from 4 a.m. back to 3 a.m.

119
00:05:41,163 --> 00:05:42,163
So 3:30

120
00:05:42,505 --> 00:05:43,505
happened twice.

121
00:05:43,535 --> 00:05:47,475
Now imagine our online shop sends
a daily sales report at 3:30,

122
00:05:48,065 --> 00:05:48,975
time.

123
00:05:48,995 --> 00:05:50,235
On one day of the year,

124
00:05:50,595 --> 00:05:53,315
that job would not run. On
another day of the year,

125
00:05:53,335 --> 00:05:55,775
it would run twice. So these
are things to consider.

126
00:05:55,795 --> 00:05:58,055
Another common edge case
is the Unicode bug.

127
00:05:58,095 --> 00:06:01,465
Unicode is the edge case that breaks
names with accents and tone marks.

128
00:06:01,575 --> 00:06:02,915
A lot of Yoruba names have this.

129
00:06:03,915 --> 00:06:07,905
Take Adébáyọ̀. Adébáyọ̀, for
example, you can see seven letters,

130
00:06:08,395 --> 00:06:11,455
but some functions count
bytes, not letters,

131
00:06:11,895 --> 00:06:16,813
right? So in this case, you would have
13, because letters like é and ọ̀ take

132
00:06:16,833 --> 00:06:21,973
more than one byte each. So if we check
the name must be at most 20 characters.

133
00:06:22,363 --> 00:06:25,463
If we check that with the wrong
function, you can reject actual names.

134
00:06:25,733 --> 00:06:29,193
So the fix for this is usually to count
with a function that counts characters

135
00:06:29,313 --> 00:06:31,393
and to normalize the text before it saves.

136
00:06:31,633 --> 00:06:34,943
Hyrum's law is about a
different kind of edge case.

137
00:06:35,073 --> 00:06:38,163
It's the behavior in your app
that you actually never promised.

138
00:06:38,453 --> 00:06:40,073
It's named after Hyrum Wright.

139
00:06:40,513 --> 00:06:41,603
Hyrum's law states

140
00:06:42,013 --> 00:06:46,093
that with a sufficient number of users
of an API, it does not matter what you

141
00:06:46,113 --> 00:06:50,618
promise in the contract. All observable
behaviors of your system will be depended

142
00:06:50,638 --> 00:06:54,780
on by someone. So let's say our online
shop API happens to return products in

143
00:06:54,800 --> 00:06:57,190
the order that we had it.
We didn't choose that.

144
00:06:57,270 --> 00:06:59,460
The query just happened
to come back that way.

145
00:06:59,655 --> 00:07:02,335
Now, if someone is using our API
to show products in their app,

146
00:07:02,415 --> 00:07:06,405
and they're showing the newest products
by taking the last product in the list,

147
00:07:06,575 --> 00:07:10,625
then if we, on our end, maybe
add an index or change the query,

148
00:07:10,735 --> 00:07:12,775
the order will change
and the app will break.

149
00:07:13,045 --> 00:07:14,675
To them, we've broken something.

150
00:07:14,855 --> 00:07:17,795
To us, we didn't break anything because
we never promised that behavior.

151
00:07:17,815 --> 00:07:18,925
But because they are used

152
00:07:19,320 --> 00:07:22,270
to getting that behavior, they now
think that we've broken something.

153
00:07:22,290 --> 00:07:23,290
So this is also

154
00:07:23,402 --> 00:07:25,902
the kind of edge case that
you also have to consider.

155
00:07:25,932 --> 00:07:27,342
To be honest, there's so much more.

156
00:07:27,362 --> 00:07:29,452
You can't undo every single one, right?

157
00:07:29,532 --> 00:07:33,112
You can't protect your app from every
single possible edge case because you

158
00:07:33,132 --> 00:07:34,692
can't even predict every single one.

159
00:07:34,712 --> 00:07:37,132
If you want to wait till you
predict every single one,

160
00:07:37,242 --> 00:07:38,532
you probably wouldn't ship anything.

161
00:07:38,672 --> 00:07:42,172
You can catch a few of them from some
of the ones that I've talked about in

162
00:07:42,192 --> 00:07:45,273
this video, but you probably wouldn't
be able to catch all of them.
